::CW_U02_UA_Q01::
[MLA.CW.NAR.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.NAR.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q01
Passage: "The apartment smelled like rain and burnt toast. Nia placed her college envelope beside the sink and waited for Grandma to notice." What is the strongest interpretation of the setting details?{
~A. They only prove breakfast happened.#Incorrect. Burnt toast suggests daily life, but the envelope and waiting create emotional pressure.
~B. They remove conflict from the scene.#Incorrect. Waiting for Grandma to notice creates tension.
=C. They place a major decision inside an ordinary home moment.#Correct. The familiar apartment details contrast with the importance of the college envelope.
~D. They show the narrator is outside.#Incorrect. The apartment and sink place the scene indoors.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q02::
[MLA.CW.NAR.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q02
Passage: "You tell yourself the stage lights are too bright to see anyone, but you still find your father's empty seat." What effect does second person create?{
=A. It pulls the reader into the character's self-directed pressure.#Correct. "You tell yourself" makes the inner moment immediate and personal.
~B. It gives every audience member a speaking role.#Incorrect. The narration focuses on one character's experience.
~C. It turns the scene into a research report.#Incorrect. The passage remains narrative. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. It removes emotion from the moment.#Incorrect. The empty seat creates emotion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q03::
[MLA.CW.FIC.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q03
Passage: A writer wants to show that a character is becoming more honest. Which action best supports that purpose?{
~A. The character changes the subject whenever asked a question.#Incorrect. That shows avoidance, not growing honesty. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The character crosses out a false sentence and writes the harder truth beneath it.#Correct. The visible revision shows movement from hiding to honesty.
~C. The character describes the weather for a full page.#Incorrect. Weather description must connect to the conflict to support the purpose.
~D. The character hides the page before anyone sees it.#Incorrect. Hiding the truth weakens the honesty arc.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q04::
[MLA.CW.FIC.03] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q04
Passage: "The bike chain slipped again at the bottom of the hill. Omar looked up at the hospital windows and started pushing." What does the action reveal?{
~A. Omar quits because the bike breaks.#Incorrect. He starts pushing, so he continues despite the problem.
~B. Omar is only interested in machines.#Incorrect. The hospital windows suggest a human reason for continuing.
~C. Omar has no goal.#Incorrect. Looking at the hospital gives direction and urgency.
=D. Omar persists because the destination matters.#Correct. The broken bike adds obstacle, and his action shows determination.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q05::
[MLA.CW.FIC.05] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q05
Passage: A draft uses a full page of backstory before the character enters the contest room. The unit skill focus is narrative scene development. What revision best improves pacing?{
=A. Begin closer to the contest room and weave only necessary background into the action.#Correct. This keeps the reader inside the important moment while preserving needed context.
~B. Add another page of unrelated backstory.#Incorrect. More delay will likely weaken the scene.
~C. Remove the contest room and keep only history.#Incorrect. The contest room creates the immediate scene pressure.
~D. Use only vague summary and no concrete moment.#Incorrect. Scene development needs specific action and detail.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q06::
[MLA.CW.NAR.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q06
Passage: "I said I did not care who won, but I checked the scoreboard before I checked my ankle." What does the narrator's behavior show?{
~A. The narrator has no conflict.#Incorrect. The contradiction creates inner conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The scoreboard is unrelated.#Incorrect. It reveals what the narrator values in the moment.
=C. The narrator's actions contradict the claim of not caring.#Correct. Checking the scoreboard first reveals hidden investment.
~D. The narrator is describing a meal.#Incorrect. The passage concerns a competition or game.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q07::
[MLA.CW.NAR.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q07
Passage: "The garden gate hung open, and the tomatoes lay crushed under one set of muddy footprints." Which technique is strongest?{
~A. Explaining every cause immediately.#Incorrect. The passage leaves readers to infer what happened.
=B. Indirectly creating a problem through setting detail.#Correct. The open gate and footprints imply an event without directly explaining it.
~C. Removing all visual detail.#Incorrect. The sentence depends on visual detail. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Switching to second person.#Incorrect. The passage is not written as "you."
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q08::
[MLA.CW.FIC.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.NAR.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q08
Passage: Which choice best states how point of view supports narrative meaning?{
~A. Point of view only decides paragraph length.#Incorrect. It shapes access to knowledge, feelings, and reliability.
~B. Point of view has no connection to character.#Incorrect. It strongly affects how readers understand character.
~C. Point of view is always third person.#Incorrect. Narratives can use first, second, or third person.
=D. Point of view controls what the reader can know and how close the reader feels to events.#Correct. Narrator position influences information, trust, and emotional distance.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q09::
[MLA.CW.FIC.03] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q09
Passage: "Mara kept the receipt for the returned dress taped inside her locker." What question would most help revise the detail for meaning?{
=A. Why does Mara keep proof of something she gave back?#Correct. This question asks how the object reveals desire, regret, or conflict.
~B. What color is every locker in the school?#Incorrect. That may add setting, but it does not connect the receipt to character meaning.
~C. How many lockers are in the hallway?#Incorrect. A count does not deepen the scene's meaning.
~D. What brand made the tape?#Incorrect. Brand detail is unlikely to matter unless connected to the conflict.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q10::
[MLA.CW.FIC.05] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q10
Passage: Scene purpose: show a character deciding to speak up. Which ending best completes the arc?{
~A. The character lowers their hand and lets someone else answer.#Incorrect. That avoids speaking up. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The character hears the room go quiet and says the first true sentence aloud.#Correct. The action shows the decision under pressure.
~C. The character leaves before the meeting starts.#Incorrect. Leaving removes the decision moment. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The character thinks about speaking but the scene ends.#Incorrect. The purpose asks for a decision, not only a thought.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q11::
[MLA.CW.NAR.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q11
Passage: "The necklace clicked against the microphone as Priya leaned forward." Which integrated effect is strongest if Priya is about to confess?{
~A. The necklace proves Priya is wealthy.#Incorrect. No evidence supports that claim. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The microphone removes the need for conflict.#Incorrect. It can make the conflict more public.
=C. The sound increases attention on the tense public moment.#Correct. The object, sound, and microphone setting all heighten the confession.
~D. The sound has no possible purpose.#Incorrect. Sound can focus mood and tension. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q12::
[MLA.CW.NAR.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q12
Passage: "Every time Leo described the fire, the match moved from the sidewalk to his own hand." What should a reader notice?{
=A. Leo's account may reveal guilt or unreliable narration.#Correct. The moving match changes responsibility and affects trust.
~B. The story is becoming more reliable.#Incorrect. Changing details often create doubt. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The setting is a beach.#Incorrect. The passage names sidewalk and fire, not beach.
~D. The sentence has no conflict.#Incorrect. The changing account creates conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q13::
[MLA.CW.FIC.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q13
Passage: A student writes, "The room was nice. The person was sad. The thing mattered." What revision would best improve craft?{
~A. Keep all vague words because they are easier.#Incorrect. Vague wording weakens scene development. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Remove the character from the scene.#Incorrect. Character is central to narrative. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Add unrelated facts about weather only.#Incorrect. Unrelated details do not solve vague craft.
=D. Replace vague words with specific details that show the room, person, and object in action.#Correct. Specific, purposeful details help readers infer emotion and meaning.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q14::
[MLA.CW.FIC.03] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q14
Passage: "The train pulled away before Elena reached the platform. She laughed, then covered her mouth with both hands." What does the reaction suggest?{
~A. Elena is definitely happy with no conflict.#Incorrect. The covered mouth complicates the laugh. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Elena feels a complicated response she is trying to control.#Correct. Laughing and covering her mouth suggest mixed emotion or surprise.
~C. Elena is not in a setting.#Incorrect. The train platform is the setting. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Elena controls the train schedule.#Incorrect. No evidence supports that. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q15::
[MLA.CW.FIC.05] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.NAR.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q15
Passage: Which option best defines an integrated narrative scene?{
~A. A scene with the longest possible paragraphs.#Incorrect. Length does not guarantee integration. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A scene with no conflict.#Incorrect. Narrative scenes usually need tension, desire, or change.
=C. A scene where point of view, character, setting, pacing, and details work together toward one purpose.#Correct. Integration means craft choices support a unified effect.
~D. A scene made only of summary.#Incorrect. Scene development uses action, setting, dialogue, and detail.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q16::
[MLA.CW.NAR.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q16
Passage: "The room went silent after I said the name. Even the aquarium filter seemed to stop." What does the aquarium detail do?{
=A. It extends the silence through a concrete sensory detail.#Correct. The filter detail makes the silence feel larger and more noticeable.
~B. It randomly changes the topic to pets.#Incorrect. The detail is connected to silence and tension.
~C. It proves the narrator is underwater.#Incorrect. An aquarium in a room does not place the narrator underwater.
~D. It removes the importance of the spoken name.#Incorrect. It emphasizes the effect of the name.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q17::
[MLA.CW.NAR.02] Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q17
Passage: A writer needs to revise a scene for standard MLA.CW.FIC.03, which focuses on character and setting. Which revision fits best?{
~A. Delete all setting so only thoughts remain.#Incorrect. The standard requires attention to setting. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Add a random object that never appears again.#Incorrect. A random object may not develop character or setting.
~C. Remove the character's decision.#Incorrect. Character development depends on meaningful choice or response.
=D. Add a setting detail that pressures the character's choice.#Correct. Setting becomes meaningful when it affects character action or understanding.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q18::
[MLA.CW.FIC.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q18
Passage: "Third-person limited follows Jade as she waits outside the office." What can the reader usually know?{
~A. Only facts from every mind in the building.#Incorrect. That describes a more omniscient approach. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Jade's experience closely, with limited access to others.#Correct. Third-person limited stays near one character while using third-person pronouns.
~C. Nothing about Jade's feelings.#Incorrect. Limited third person can reveal Jade's thoughts and feelings.
~D. The reader becomes the character through "you."#Incorrect. That is second person. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q19::
[MLA.CW.FIC.03] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q19
Passage: "The empty chair at the end of the table stayed pulled out all dinner." What does the detail most likely suggest?{
~A. The table is too small for plates.#Incorrect. The passage focuses on absence, not table size.
~B. The chair has no meaning.#Incorrect. Its placement and emphasis make it meaningful.
=C. A missing person or unresolved absence affects the meal.#Correct. The pulled-out empty chair draws attention to someone not there.
~D. Everyone ignored the chair successfully.#Incorrect. The sentence itself keeps attention on it.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q20::
[MLA.CW.FIC.05] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q20
Passage: A draft has the line, "I was nervous." Which revision best shows rather than only tells?{
=A. I counted the same three floor tiles until my name was called.#Correct. The repeated counting shows nervousness through action.
~B. I was very, very nervous.#Incorrect. This intensifies telling but does not show behavior.
~C. Nervous means worried.#Incorrect. Defining the word does not create a scene.
~D. The room was a room.#Incorrect. This adds no meaningful detail. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q21::
[MLA.CW.NAR.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q21
Passage: "The narrator refuses to explain why she hides the medal, but she checks the drawer every night." What is the best reader inference?{
~A. The medal is definitely worthless.#Incorrect. Her repeated checking suggests value. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The medal carries unresolved emotional importance.#Correct. Hiding and checking it repeatedly show it matters even without full explanation.
~C. The drawer is the main character.#Incorrect. The drawer is part of the action, not the character.
~D. The narrator has forgotten the medal.#Incorrect. She checks it nightly. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q22::
[MLA.CW.NAR.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.NAR.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q22
Passage: Which feedback best helps a student whose scene has many details but no clear purpose?{
~A. Add more unrelated details.#Incorrect. More unrelated detail increases confusion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Remove all concrete detail.#Incorrect. The goal is purposeful detail, not no detail.
=C. Choose the scene's main change, then keep or revise details that support that change.#Correct. Purpose guides which details belong and how they should function.
~D. Change every sentence to a question.#Incorrect. Sentence form alone will not solve purpose.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q23::
[MLA.CW.FIC.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q23
Passage: "When the principal asked who painted the mural, Sam looked at the blue stain on his sleeve." What does the stain do?{
~A. It proves Sam is unrelated to the mural.#Incorrect. It suggests the opposite. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. It changes the narration to first person.#Incorrect. The sentence remains third person. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. It makes the scene about laundry only.#Incorrect. Laundry is not the central conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. It reveals evidence through a small character detail.#Correct. The stain connects Sam physically to the mural.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q24::
[MLA.CW.FIC.03] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q24
Passage: "The doorbell rang once. Nobody moved. It rang again, and Aunt Celia set down the carving knife." What pacing technique is used?{
=A. Repetition and pause build suspense before action.#Correct. The repeated ring and delayed movement stretch tension.
~B. Summary skips the moment entirely.#Incorrect. The passage slows the moment. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A flashback explains childhood.#Incorrect. No flashback appears. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The scene becomes informational writing.#Incorrect. It remains narrative. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q25::
[MLA.CW.FIC.05] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q25
Passage: Which final revision question best prepares a scene for unit mastery?{
~A. Did I use the most words possible?#Incorrect. More words do not automatically improve craft.
~B. Did I remove every concrete detail?#Incorrect. Concrete details are essential when purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Did I avoid revising the scene?#Incorrect. Revision is part of mastery. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Do my point of view, character choices, setting details, pacing, and key objects all support the same intended effect?#Correct. This checks the major Unit 2 craft skills together.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q26::
[MLA.CW.NAR.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.NAR.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q26
Which choice best identifies a narrative technique in this sentence? "Maya pressed her ear to the door and held her breath."{
~A. Dialogue#Incorrect. Dialogue means exact spoken words. This sentence does not show anyone speaking.
~B. Reflection#Incorrect. Reflection shows a thought, realization, or interpretation. This sentence focuses on what Maya does.
=C. Action#Correct. Maya presses her ear to the door and holds her breath. These are actions that show the reader what she is doing.
~D. Transition#Incorrect. A transition shows movement between moments, such as time, cause, place, or contrast. This sentence is not mainly connecting two moments.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q27::
[MLA.CW.NAR.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.NAR.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q27
Which revision best turns the event list into a scene? Event list: "I opened the box. I saw the medal. I cried."{
~A. I opened the box, saw the medal, and cried.#Incorrect. This keeps the event list compressed. It tells what happened but does not develop the moment as a scene.
=B. I lifted the cardboard flap and found the silver medal wrapped in a blue ribbon. For a moment, I could not move. Then my eyes filled with tears.#Correct. This revision uses action, description, and a time transition to let the reader experience the moment.
~C. First, I opened the box. Next, I saw the medal. Finally, I cried.#Incorrect. This adds mechanical transitions, but it still reads like a list rather than a developed scene.
~D. The medal was important because I cried after opening the box.#Incorrect. This explains the event instead of showing it through narrative techniques.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q28::
[MLA.CW.FIC.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.NAR.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q28
Which transition best shows cause in this sentence? "_____ the hallway lights went out, Jordan reached for the wall."{
=A. Because#Correct. "Because the hallway lights went out" explains the cause of Jordan reaching for the wall.
~B. Across from#Incorrect. "Across from" shows place, not cause. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Later that day#Incorrect. "Later that day" shows time, not cause.
~D. Meanwhile#Incorrect. "Meanwhile" shows simultaneous action, not cause. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q29::
[MLA.CW.FIC.03] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q29
Which sentence is written in first person point of view?{
~A. Lena opened the drawer and held her breath.#Incorrect. This is third person because the narrator uses Lena's name.
~B. She opened the drawer and held her breath.#Incorrect. This is third person because the narrator uses she.
=C. I opened the drawer and held my breath.#Correct. The narrator uses I and my, so the narrator is inside the story.
~D. They opened the drawer and held their breath.#Incorrect. This is third person because the narrator uses they and their.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q30::
[MLA.CW.FIC.05] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q30
Which choice correctly explains third person limited point of view?{
~A. The narrator uses I and tells only personal experience.#Incorrect. That describes first person point of view.
~B. The narrator directly reveals every character's thoughts at any time.#Incorrect. That describes omniscient narration, not limited narration.
~C. The narrator never reveals any thoughts or feelings.#Incorrect. Third person limited can reveal one character's thoughts and feelings.
=D. The narrator uses names or third person pronouns and stays close to one character's thoughts.#Correct. Third person limited is outside the story but limited to one focus character's experience.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q31::
[MLA.CW.NAR.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q31
Passage: "Mina kept the bent bus pass in her coat pocket even after she moved close enough to walk to school." Which key element is most important?{
~A. Dialogue#Incorrect. No character speaks in the passage. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Object detail#Correct. The bent bus pass is a physical object that suggests memory, habit, or attachment.
~C. Plot summary#Incorrect. The passage shows a detail, but the question asks for the key element doing craft work.
~D. Point of view shift#Incorrect. There is no shift in narrator perspective.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q32::
[MLA.CW.NAR.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q32
Passage: "The room was empty except for a chair turned toward the wall." What does the setting detail most likely add?{
=A. It creates mystery by implying something happened before the scene began.#Correct. The unusual chair position invites the reader to infer a prior event.
~B. It proves the character is happy.#Incorrect. The passage does not provide evidence of happiness.
~C. It removes tension from the scene.#Incorrect. The odd detail creates tension rather than removing it.
~D. It explains the entire backstory.#Incorrect. The detail suggests meaning but does not explain the whole backstory.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q33::
[MLA.CW.FIC.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q33
Passage: "I set the cracked mug beside the new one and reached for the cracked one again." What is the literal meaning?{
~A. The narrator moves to a new city.#Incorrect. The passage does not mention moving. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The narrator dislikes all mugs.#Incorrect. The narrator chooses a mug, so this is not supported.
=C. The narrator chooses an old cracked mug instead of a new mug.#Correct. Literal meaning states what directly happens on the surface.
~D. The narrator is definitely angry.#Incorrect. That is an unsupported inference. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q34::
[MLA.CW.FIC.03] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q34
Passage: "The graduation banner stretched across the living room, but the suitcase by the stairs was already packed." Which evidence creates contrast?{
~A. Both details show the same exact mood.#Incorrect. The point is that the details pull in different directions.
=B. The banner suggests celebration, while the packed suitcase suggests leaving.#Correct. The two details carry different emotional meanings, creating contrast.
~C. The suitcase proves no one graduated.#Incorrect. The passage does not support that. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The banner is not a detail.#Incorrect. The banner is an important detail. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q35::
[MLA.CW.FIC.05] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q35
Passage: "I was clearly the calmest person in the room. True, I had knocked over two chairs and shouted at the clock, but that only proved everyone else was making me nervous." What creates irony?{
~A. The narrator uses no evidence.#Incorrect. The passage gives evidence: knocked over chairs and shouted at a clock.
~B. The narrator is completely reliable.#Incorrect. The narrator's claim conflicts with the evidence.
~C. The narrator uses third person point of view.#Incorrect. The passage uses first person. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. The narrator claims calmness while the evidence shows panic.#Correct. The gap between claim and evidence creates irony.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q36::
[MLA.CW.NAR.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q36
Passage: "To prove our club valued student voices, the officers created a suggestion box, locked it, lost the key, and congratulated themselves for listening." What is the satirical target?{
=A. Fake listening or performative leadership#Correct. The club claims to listen while making suggestions impossible to read.
~B. The color of the suggestion box#Incorrect. Color is not mentioned. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A serious natural disaster#Incorrect. The passage targets behavior in a club, not a disaster.
~D. A reliable narrator's careful honesty#Incorrect. The passage criticizes a contradiction in leadership behavior.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q37::
[MLA.CW.NAR.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q37
Passage: "The phone lit up. I reached for it, stopped, and watched my name reflect faintly in the black screen." What pacing choice is used?{
~A. Compression of unimportant events#Incorrect. The passage slows a single important moment rather than compressing it.
=B. Expansion of an important moment#Correct. The writer slows the phone moment with action and visual detail.
~C. A complete summary of a month#Incorrect. The passage focuses on one moment. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. No pacing choice#Incorrect. The slowing is a clear pacing choice.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q38::
[MLA.CW.FIC.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q38
Which revision creates more tension? Draft: "I opened the door."{
~A. I opened the door.#Incorrect. This is unchanged and does not add tension.
~B. Doors are common in buildings.#Incorrect. This is general information, not tension. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. I opened the door slowly because the last text had said, "Do not go inside yet."#Correct. The warning creates stakes and makes the reader wait for what is inside.
~D. The door was a door.#Incorrect. This adds no stakes or pacing. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q39::
[MLA.CW.FIC.03] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q39
Passage: Maya stood outside the closed gym doors with the debate trophy under one arm. Inside, her teammates were laughing. She had won the final round, but she had also ignored Kai's signal to let him answer the last question. Which choice best shows an integrated scene plan?{
~A. Purpose: show Maya is happy; Point of view: any; Key element: trophy; Pacing: fast only.#Incorrect. This names pieces, but they do not explain the conflict or how the reader should understand Maya's choice.
~B. Purpose: describe the gym; Point of view: narrator far away; Key element: doors; Pacing: many descriptions.#Incorrect. The gym may matter, but the central pressure is Maya's win and her damaged teamwork.
~C. Purpose: explain debate rules; Point of view: second person; Key element: final question; Pacing: summary.#Incorrect. Debate rules are background. The scene needs choices that reveal character, conflict, and meaning.
=D. Purpose: show Maya realizing success can still cost trust; Point of view: close third on Maya; Key element: trophy pressed against her side; Pacing: slow at the doors, then faster when she enters.#Correct. This connects purpose, narrator focus, key element, and pacing so each craft choice supports the same meaning.
}

::CW_U02_UA_Q40::
[MLA.CW.FIC.05] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.01
Question ID: CW_U02_UA_Q40
Passage: The narrator says, "I kept the cracked compass in my pocket because Dad said it always found the truth. When it spun wildly beside the old train station, I almost turned back." What is the strongest scene purpose?{
=A. To show a character facing uncertainty through a meaningful object.#Correct. The compass, the memory of Dad, and the choice to continue all work together to create purpose.
~B. To list all the places the character has traveled.#Incorrect. The passage focuses on one meaningful moment, not a travel list.
~C. To prove the compass is scientifically accurate.#Incorrect. The compass works symbolically in the scene; the important issue is the character's decision and fear.
~D. To describe the train station architecture in detail.#Incorrect. The station is setting, but the passage does not make architecture the main purpose.
}
